# An RPKI validator mirrors every RRDP response it fetches to a local copy of
# the repository tree. src/collector/rrdp.rs's HttpResponse::open_file derives
# the mirror location from the request URI: strip the scheme and join what is
# left -- host and path -- onto the operator-configured response directory,
# then create the parent directories and the file. The URIs it is handed are
# not the operator's: they come out of the notification file and delta list
# the RRDP server itself publishes, so a repository decides both the host and
# the path of a file that lands under the local mirror root. A URI whose path
# carries `..` segments walks the join out of the mirror directory, and the
# write happens with the validator's privileges -- path traversal, CWE-22.
#
# The fixed revision moves the path construction into a helper that walks the URI's
# segments and refuses `.` and `..`, and confirms the joined path still starts
# at the base. The storing function keeps the directory creation and the file
# create but does not join the URI itself.
#
# What fires without this change: nothing. The tree reports 46 findings, all
# NullableDeref noise and one WeakRandomValue, with not one PATH row and
# nothing at all inside open_file. The fs::File::create call is labelled
# code.FilePathAccess (rust.sinks filePathAccess107), but no Rust source
# concept can reach the URI: the notification file is parsed by the external
# rpki crate, so the URIs enter this crate constructed, and the only
# Rust sources are env/args and web-framework entry parameters.
#
# Nothing is minted. The concept (code.RemoteListingDownloadPath) and the rule
# (VYQL-PATH-006) exist and cover exactly this weakness -- a
# remote listing's names joined into local download paths -- for C (scp/ftp
# remote filenames) and JavaScript. The addition is the Rust binding
# vyql/bindings/rust/remote_uri_mirror_path_join.vyql: a function that joins
# the string form of a URI or URL into a local path and materialises that path
# with a file or directory creation, without any of std's containment or
# traversal checks in the same function. The neutralising markers are std's
# own vocabulary -- starts_with / strip_prefix containment, a components()
# walk, ParentDir / CurDir arms, canonicalize, is_absolute, a sanitizer, or a
# literal "." / ".." segment comparison -- so a fix written in place is
# honoured, not just the helper-extraction shape this repository happened to
# choose.
#
# Measured. The vulnerable revision of src/collector/rrdp.rs reports open_file
# as VYQL-PATH-006 and the patched revision reports none, three --cache off
# runs each; renaming the fixed revision's get_mirror_path helper in the patched tree
# changes nothing on either side, so the discrimination rides on the
# join/create co-location and the std containment markers, not on the fixed revision's
# names. Precision, enumerated rather than sampled: every Rust file under the
# sibling clones that names a join and a create call and any uri/url marker
# (8 files across 5 trees, from 4816 .rs files in 299 clones) plus the four
# largest Rust trees scanned whole -- one true positive (the target) and no
# other row. A first revision of the binding also matched the Display render
# spellings (to_string, and to_str as its prefix): apollo-router's changeset
# tool renders p.url.to_string() into markdown in the same function that joins
# a locally typed slug, which read as a second row and is not a remote name
# reaching a path. Dropping the render spellings removed it; the borrow
# accessors (as_str, path) are what a join actually consumes.

test "rank1688: (a) a URI's string form joined into a mirror path and written with no containment check"
  lang rust
  file src/collector/rrdp.rs
  expect VYQL-PATH-006
  code
  ```
  use std::fs;
  use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

  struct HttpResponse;

  impl HttpResponse {
      fn open_file(
          base: &Path, uri: &uri::Https, multi: bool
      ) -> Result<fs::File, Failed> {
          let path = base.join(&uri.as_str()[8..]);
          let path = if multi {
              path.join(Utc::now().to_rfc3339())
          }
          else {
              path
          };

          let parent = match path.parent() {
              Some(parent) => parent,
              None => {
                  return Err(Failed)
              }
          };
          if let Err(_err) = fs::create_dir_all(parent) {
              return Err(Failed)
          }
          fs::File::create(&path).map_err(|_err| {
              Failed
          })
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1688: (b) the patched helper rejects dot segments and requires the joined path to stay at the base"
  lang rust
  file src/collector/rrdp.rs
  reject VYQL-PATH-006
  code
  ```
  use std::fs;
  use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

  struct HttpResponse;

  impl HttpResponse {
      fn open_file(
          base: &Path, uri: &uri::Https, multi: bool
      ) -> Option<fs::File> {
          let path = Self::get_mirror_path(base, uri, multi)?;
          let parent = match path.parent() {
              Some(parent) => parent,
              None => {
                  return None
              }
          };
          if let Err(_err) = fs::create_dir_all(parent) {
              return None
          }
          fs::File::create(&path).map_err(|_err| {
              Failed
          }).ok()
      }

      fn get_mirror_path(
          base: &Path, uri: &uri::Https, multi: bool
      ) -> Option<PathBuf> {
          let rel = &uri.as_str()[8..];
          for segment in rel.split('/') {
              if segment == "." || segment == ".." {
                  return None
              }
          }
          let path = base.join(rel);
          if !path.starts_with(base) {
              return None
          }
          if multi {
              Some(path.join(Utc::now().to_rfc3339()))
          }
          else {
              Some(path)
          }
      }
  }
  ```

